await writeFile(L25+'notes.md',`l25 changes only dust from l24 on the accepted l21 stellar model. It is a diagnostic, not a promoted model. Stellar density, point fluxes and colors, source gains, all component grades and the display curve remain unchanged.\n\nOGLE red-clump reddening sets regional effective foreground fractions. Its released text cells are 3.4–27.5 arcminutes wide; the central effective sampling can be finer because their grids overlap. Chen's mean Milky Way foreground is E(B-V)=0.06 mag. Its regional maps are unavailable checks, as the owner approved. The saved NADC login HTML is never treated as FITS data.\n\nHERITAGE retains its native 56 arcsecond columns and the single l24 Bell factor of 0.885809. The global dust strength is ${num(result.dustStrength).toFixed(1)}, within declared bounds of 0.8–1.2. There is no local Bell normalization or random clump spread. Under the adopted old-bar tracer, the column-weighted foreground fraction in the supported bar samples is ${(100*num(native.oldTracerMeanFrontFraction)).toFixed(1)} percent.\n\n| Masked bar close-up | R/G | B/G |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n${row('Horálek',colors.photo)}\n${row('l21',colors.l21)}\n${row('l24',colors.l24)}\n${row('l25',colors.l25)}\n\nThe l25 bar color errors are ${errors.map(e=>(100*e).toFixed(2)+'%').join(' and ')}.\n\n| Photo lane pixels versus adjacent bar | Relative R/G | Relative B/G | Relative green brightness | Lane overlap |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n${laneRow('Horálek',lanes.photo)}\n${laneRow('l21',lanes.l21)}\n${laneRow('l24',lanes.l24)}\n${laneRow('l25',lanes.l25)}\n\nThe overlap is intersection over union of independently detected brown, dark lanes in the same masked bar. ${(100*num(location.photoBrightFractionOfModelLanes)).toFixed(2)} percent of l25's detected lane pixels fall in the photo's bright, blue-white quarter. These are image-morphology checks, not dust-distance measurements. Full masks and definitions are saved in lane-checks.json.\n\nThe regional columns disagree substantially: ${num(dust.clippedRegions)} of ${num(dust.regions)} overlapping measurement boxes reach a fraction bound; ${num(dust.aboveTotalRegions)} exceed the total HERITAGE column before clipping. No extra column is invented to hide that disagreement. The front-fraction interpretation and three-dimensional placement remain assumptions, especially outside the old bar.\n\nTraining and held-out RMS are ${num(m.trainRelativeRms).toFixed(6)} and ${num(m.heldoutRelativeRms).toFixed(6)}. RMS is not the acceptance gate. The actual finite-patch total column agrees with HERITAGE to ${(100*num(native.relativeRms)).toFixed(4)} percent RMS in supported bar samples before the final global strength. At strength 1.2, the total column is 20 percent above the Bell-normalized unity reference.\n\nThree bounded trials are recorded. The inspected result still lacks the connected brown-orange lanes and blue-white intervals of the photo. Black punctures are reduced, but remain beside bright gas. Lane location improves slightly; lane color is effectively unchanged. The side views stay coherent. The visual lane criterion fails despite passing average bar color. No full-resolution fit or slab bake ran.\n`);
await writeFile(L25+'method.md',`[Skowron et al. 2021](https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4365/abcb81) measures red-clump E(V-I), including Milky Way foreground. The four author-released text tables supply 81,684 overlapping adaptive cells. A query selects the finest containing Hammer-projection square and then the nearest centre. The actual text-cell width is reported, rather than claiming 56 arcsecond stellar reddening. All 86,690 valid HERITAGE samples have regional OGLE support.\n\nThe internal stellar column is A_V,RC = 3.41 max(0, E(V-I)/1.318 - 0.06). The E(V-I)/E(B-V) conversion is the optical comparison adopted in Skowron's paper; R_V=3.41 is the Gordon LMC value. The constant foreground is the LMC mean from [Chen et al. 2022](https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stac072). Chen combines several surveys, including VMC; it is not exclusively a red-clump map. Its regional products could not be retrieved and are not claimed as numerical checks. The owner explicitly approved this exception.\n\nThe total column is the full [Gordon et al. 2014 HERITAGE BEMBB expectation map](https://doi.org/10.1088/0004-637X/797/2/85), converted by A_V=7.394 Sigma_d using [Bell et al. 2022 equation 1](https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stac1545), and multiplied by l24's one galaxy-wide Bell factor. Within each full published OGLE square, the solid-angle-weighted mean of all measured HERITAGE samples is the denominator for the effective fraction A_V,RC/A_V,total. This ratio determines depth ordering; it never rescales individual native columns. Fractions are limited to 0.02–0.98 to keep finite depths. Saturation and conflicting totals are counted explicitly. The source RC distribution widths describe differential reddening and stellar scatter; they are not treated as errors on the regional median.\n\nFor geometry, an adopted Gaussian old-bar tracer has sigma 367 pc. Five fixed positive Gaussian quadrature nodes represent the unchanged 250 pc dust depth distribution. The regional mean height solves the quadrature-weighted probability that an old star lies behind the dust. A 50 pc Gaussian blends the regional mean-height transitions. This converts an effective mean column into a spatial ordering; neither OGLE nor Chen measures those individual dust distances. The RC median-to-mean approximation, optical selection bias, use of a bar tracer beyond the bar, and far-side correlation remain limitations.\n\nEach native HERITAGE patch has compact sky and line-of-sight widths of about 13.5 pc. Its five weighted copies occupy the fixed depth nodes at the measured sky position, preserving the native projected column. Their widths and integrated mass are positive. They are a numerical approximation to the adopted depth distribution, not five observed physical sheets. No random displacement, camera-conditional opacity or per-pixel photo transfer is used. The same finite 3D objects attenuate every camera. This depth correlation is a prior; the Earth image cannot determine the far side. The first trial instead spread the source map through a normal-direction Gaussian and erased the narrow filaments, so it was rejected at tiny size.\n\nThe renderer keeps the existing [Gordon et al. 2003 LMC extinction prescription](https://doi.org/10.1086/376774): R_V=3.41 and fixed red/green/blue A_lambda/A_V proxies of 0.75, 1 and 1+1/3.41. These are broad optical channel approximations, not a measured Horálek camera response or an arbitrary lane tint. The sole calibrated scalar is dust strength, bounded to 0.8–1.2. Three corrected tiny bar evaluations minimize lane-to-adjacent R/G and B/G ratio errors, subject to the 2 percent bar color condition. Its final value is ${num(result.dustStrength).toFixed(1)}. Stellar grades and the display curve are frozen.\n\nPhoto lane pixels provide only a global color calibration and an independent spatial check. A 1-to-8 arcminute mask-normalized contrast statistic identifies red excess and green-light deficits within a catalogue-masked bar. Identical photo lane and adjacent-light masks measure all versions. Separately detected model masks provide overlap and false-darkening statistics. Source pixels, lane masks and residuals never enter the volume construction. The diagnostic detects a displaced-lane control with unchanged color histogram.\n`);
